From e2768d1da61daa8a2baebf2a573eac7ef5bebc39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duckfamily <156525766+duckfamily@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 02:32:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 01/13] Seal the local key to the machine's TPM

Upstream runs PBKDF2 with a single iteration when no local passcode is set,
so the local key comes essentially from a salt stored in plaintext next to
the data. Copying the tdata folder is enough to read everything.

TpmSeed() signs a digest inside a non-exportable RSA key held by the CNG
platform provider and returns a digest of the signature. PKCS#1 v1.5 is used
deliberately: it has no randomised padding, so the same input always gives
the same signature, which is what allows the result to be key material
rather than a yes/no answer software could step past.

Domain::startModern falls back to the unsealed derivation when the seeded
one cannot open an existing key file, then re-seals it. Without that, a
machine that gains a usable TPM after first run would show a passcode prompt
the person never set and lose their local history.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
